ic backlash because their  country  is currently in a partial lockdown  though they didn't violate lockdown  rules  the netherlands discourages any  unnecessary travel  still ahead a new treatment to battle  blindness it may open the door for other  conditions  a groundbreaking gene therapy that can  restore eyesight has now been approved  in canada  it's a major development for people who  have lost vision  due to certain genetically inherited eye  diseases  and assolin go reports this opens the  door for gene therapy to be used to  treat  or prevent other diseases jack mccormick  was born with a faulty gene that's  causing his vision to deteriorate  by high school he couldn't see faces  unless they were right next to him  a few years ago he heard about a  promising treatment in the works  i remember thinking wow like imagine if  this could help me one day and it turns  out it can  and that's really exciting for me health  canada has approved  luxterna a gene therapy that can restore  sight for people with retinitis  pigmentosa  or liber congenital amaurosis if their  mutation is  in a certain gene the treatment is a  surgical delivery  of a genetically modified  virus that carries a normal copy of the  gene  into the eye dr ian mcdonald says in the  ophthalmology world  this is the equivalent of walking on the  moon the copy of the normal gene  replaces the defective one  and starts making the protein the eyes  need before  treatment this patient struggles to walk  through a maze in dim lighting  and one year after treatment the same  test is a breeze  wow they could easily navigate step over  obstacles we're seeing such amazing  results  uh people being able to get their night  vision back  being able to navigate again having  their independence  it's just truly an amazing moment donors  to fighting blindness canada  funded some of the early research that  led to luxterna  now they're hoping the provinces will do  their part by covering it for patients  the one-time treatment is expected to  cost over a million dollars  but the charity says that investment  will save health care  costs in the long run so in the room  right now i can see the cabinet that's  behind me  but i wouldn't be able to necessarily  see say the handles  on it jack hopes to at least save what  little vision he has left  the belief was there's nothing that  could be done about it but that's  changing every day for so many people  it's giving a lot of people hope for the  future  sulingo global news edmonton  up next in a tent for seven months and  counting all for a cause  [Music]  how's this for a balancing act a new  world record has been set in germany and  it's not for the faint of heart  this slackliner crossed the max morlock  stadium in germany at a height of 73  meters  he walked across from one floodlight  pole to another  for a distance of 230 meters breaking  the world record for the longest high  line  in a stadium to another kind  of amazing feat anyone who's ever cooked  up a thanksgiving feast knows  how much work goes into it well a  hamilton teenager has taken over the  task for his family  and the final product is bringing joy to  thousands  this photo of rehan audu posted on  twitter by his older brother  has received hundreds of thousands of  likes the 14 year old spent  two days cooking this meal all on his  own  he started helping his mother with  thanksgiving dinner when he was just  10 but he's since taken over the  delicious deed  a ten-year-old british boy is getting  plenty of attention for a good deed  and he's having quite an adventure while  he's at it he's paying tribute to his  late neighbor by pursuing his passion  for the outdoors  and paying it forward to an organization  that provides comfort  to those who are dying crystal  commansing has that story  spending time outdoors is something  we're all trying to do  as much as possible these days for a  ten-year-old boy a tent  in the backyard is a wonderful bit of  privacy  a pop-up refuge but this isn't a typical  campout it's a tribute he was super fit  he loved the outdoors  max wussey is talking about rick abbott  he used to live next door  he and max were buddies he'd be doing  active things like going to the gym  or going on the water sports like  kayaking and paddleboarding  abbott passed away but how he lived  stirred something in his young neighbor  he started a couple of days after the  beginning of lockdown because we  actually said no  to when he asked the first few nights so  he's been out there since towards the  end of march  this campout is raising tens of  thousands of dollars for  the north devon hospice that helped care  for his friend  the chief executive of the facility  stephen roberts  says max's exploits have been a rare ray  of sunshine  max is an absolute hospice hero  and we're also proud of what he's  achieved  i'm planning to do a year in the tent  and then see where it goes from there  really  then he's coming inside no more i'm not  okay so no end date has been set just  yet  but his mum rachel is proud it started  out just kind of raising money  and raising awareness that actually  these charities are still there and  they're working harder than ever  but their funding has been cut so it's  just sort of everybody needs to support  their local communities  as you can see it's very cold  and very windy max is just a few feet  from a warm  comfortable bed in a room with sturdy  walls  but here inside his tent he's helping to  make life more  comfortable for others while fulfilling  a promise to his friend  he is having an adventure  crystal commencing global news london  there are good people in this world and  that is global national for this sunday  i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is gravelberg  saskatchewan thank you for watching  donna friesen will be here tomorrow have  a great night  you
 

